  "question": "What is the smallest positive integer that leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by each of 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6, and is exactly divisible by 7? Show your reasoning, then give the final answer on its own line as 'ANSWER: <number>'.",
  "expected": "301",
  "correct": true,
  "reasoning_tokens": 217,
  "answer_tokens": 191,
  "completion_tokens": 410,
  "ttft_s": 0.2670569389592856,
  "elapsed_s": 7.040638076141477,
  "decode_tok_s": 60.529281586277705,
  "finish_reason": "stop",
  "reasoning_text": "We need answer math. Need smallest positive integer that leaves remainder 1 when divided by 2,3,4,5,6 and divisible by 7. So N \u2261 1 mod lcm(2,3,4,5,6)=60. N \u2261 0 mod 7. Need smallest positive N = 60k + 1 divisible by 7. 60 \u2261 4 mod 7, so 4k + 1 \u2261 0 mod 7 => 4k \u2261 6 mod 7. Inverse of 4 mod 7 is 2 (4*2=8\u22611), so k \u2261 12 \u2261 5 mod 7. Smallest k=5. N=60*5+1=301. Check 301/7=43. Remainder 1 when divided by 2? 301 odd yes; by 3 sum digits 4 -> remainder 1; by 4 301 mod4=1; by5=1; by6=1. Answer 301.",
  "answer_text": "The number must be of the form \\(60k + 1\\) because it leaves remainder 1 when divided by 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6, whose least common multiple is 60.\n\nIt must also be divisible by 7:\n\\[\n60k + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSince \\(60 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}\\):\n\\[\n4k + 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\n4k \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nMultiplying by the inverse of 4 modulo 7, which is 2:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 12 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nThe smallest positive \\(k\\) is 5, so:\n\\[\n60(5) + 1 = 301\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 301"
 },
